<section id="rtn-oosqlSql"><title>oosqlSql</title>
<indexterm><primary>oosqlSql</primary></indexterm>
<indexterm><primary>Classic Rexx Interface</primary><secondary>oosqlSql</secondary></indexterm>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject>
<imagedata fileref="images/classic/oosqlsql.svg" scale="55" />
</imageobject>
</mediaobject>

<para>
  Returns the SQL text of a prepared statement.
</para>
<variablelist>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Arguments:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The only argument is:
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry><term>stmt [required]</term>
      <listitem>
      <para>
        The <link linkend="defHandle">handle</link> to a statement prepared by <link
        linkend="rtn-oosqlPrepare">oosqlPrepare</link>. The handle must not be null, and the statement must not have
        been finalized: see the rules for <link linkend="sctClassicStmt">statement handles</link>.
      </para>
      </listitem></varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Return value:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The text of the SQL statement, as it was given to <link linkend="rtn-oosqlPrepare">oosqlPrepare</link>. When the
    text given to <emphasis role="italic">oosqlPrepare</emphasis> held more than one statement, only the first one is
    returned, because only that one was prepared.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Remarks:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The text does not change when values are bound to the parameters: the parameters are still shown as they were
    written.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Details</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The functionality of the <emphasis role="italic">oosqlSql</emphasis> routine is similar to that of the SQLite <ulink
    url="http://www.sqlite.org/c3ref/expanded_sql.html">sqlite3_sql</ulink> API.
  </para>
  <para>
    The <link linkend="mthSqlClsStmt">sql</link> method of the <link linkend="clsOOSQLiteStmt">ooSQLiteStmt</link> class
    does the same for a statement object.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Example:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    This example executes, one by one, all the statements in a string. It uses the <emphasis
    role="italic">_tail</emphasis> argument of <link linkend="rtn-oosqlPrepare">oosqlPrepare</link> to get what follows
    each statement, and displays each statement before executing it:
<programlisting language="rexx">
  sql = "CREATE TABLE t(a); INSERT INTO t VALUES(1); INSERT INTO t VALUES(2);"
  do while sql &lt;&gt; ''
    rc = oosqlPrepare(db, sql~strip, 'stmt', 'sql')
    if rc &lt;&gt; .ooSQLite~OK then leave
    if oosqlIsHandleNull(stmt) then leave  -- only blanks or comments were left
    say oosqlSql(stmt)
    rc = oosqlStep(stmt)
    call oosqlFinalize stmt
  end
  /* Output:
  CREATE TABLE t(a);
  INSERT INTO t VALUES(1);
  INSERT INTO t VALUES(2);
  */
</programlisting>
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</section>  <!-- End oosqlSql() -->


