<section id="rtn-oosqlStep"><title>oosqlStep</title>
<indexterm><primary>oosqlStep</primary></indexterm>
<indexterm><primary>Classic Rexx Interface</primary><secondary>oosqlStep</secondary></indexterm>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject>
<imagedata fileref="images/classic/oosqlstep.svg" scale="55" />
</imageobject>
</mediaobject>

<para>
  Executes a prepared statement, or advances it to its next result row.
</para>
<variablelist>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Arguments:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The only argument is:
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry><term>stmt [required]</term>
      <listitem>
      <para>
        The <link linkend="defHandle">handle</link> to a statement prepared by <link
        linkend="rtn-oosqlPrepare">oosqlPrepare</link>. The handle must not be null, and the statement must not have
        been finalized: see the rules for <link linkend="sctClassicStmt">statement handles</link>.
      </para>
      </listitem></varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Return value:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    One of the <link linkend="sctResultCode">Result Code</link> constants. The most common ones are
    <computeroutput>ROW</computeroutput>, when a new result row is ready, and its values can be retrieved with the
    <emphasis role="italic">oosqlColumn</emphasis> routines; <computeroutput>DONE</computeroutput>, when the statement
    has run to completion: there are no more rows, or, for a statement that returns no data, it has been executed; and
    <computeroutput>BUSY</computeroutput>, when the database is locked by another connection, and the statement can be
    stepped again later, see <link linkend="rtn-oosqlBusyTimeOut">oosqlBusyTimeOut</link> and <link
    linkend="rtn-oosqlBusyHandler">oosqlBusyHandler</link>.
  </para>
  <para>
    Any other code is an error, which <link linkend="rtn-oosqlErrMsg">oosqlErrMsg</link> describes. ooSQLite enables
    extended result codes for every connection, so the code is often an extended one, such as
    <computeroutput>CONSTRAINT_UNIQUE</computeroutput> (2067) for a duplicate value in a UNIQUE column, rather than
    <computeroutput>CONSTRAINT</computeroutput> (19).
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Remarks:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    A statement that returns data, such as a SELECT, is stepped once for each result row, and once more to get
    <computeroutput>DONE</computeroutput>. A statement that returns no data, such as an INSERT, is executed by a single
    step, which returns <computeroutput>DONE</computeroutput> on success. After <computeroutput>DONE</computeroutput> or
    an error, do not step the statement again before <link linkend="rtn-oosqlReset">resetting</link> it.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Details</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The functionality of the <emphasis role="italic">oosqlStep</emphasis> routine is similar to that of the SQLite
    <ulink url="http://www.sqlite.org/c3ref/step.html">sqlite3_step</ulink> API.
  </para>
  <para>
    The <link linkend="mthStepClsStmt">step</link> method of the <link linkend="clsOOSQLiteStmt">ooSQLiteStmt</link>
    class does the same for a statement object.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Example:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    This example displays all the rows of a query, and checks that it ended without error:
<programlisting language="rexx">
  rc = oosqlPrepare(db, "SELECT name FROM foods ORDER BY name;", 'stmt')
  do forever
    rc = oosqlStep(stmt)
    if rc &lt;&gt; .ooSQLite~ROW then leave
    say oosqlColumnText(stmt, 1)
  end
  if rc &lt;&gt; .ooSQLite~DONE then say 'Error' rc':' oosqlErrMsg(db)
  call oosqlFinalize stmt
</programlisting>
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</section>  <!-- End oosqlStep() -->


<section id="rtn-oosqlStmtBusy"><title>oosqlStmtBusy</title>
<indexterm><primary>oosqlStmtBusy</primary></indexterm>
<indexterm><primary>Classic Rexx Interface</primary><secondary>oosqlStmtBusy</secondary></indexterm>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject>
<imagedata fileref="images/classic/oosqlstmtbusy.svg" scale="55" />
</imageobject>
</mediaobject>

<para>
  Tests if a prepared statement has been stepped, but has not run to completion and has not been reset.
</para>
<variablelist>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Arguments:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The only argument is:
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry><term>stmt [required]</term>
      <listitem>
      <para>
        The <link linkend="defHandle">handle</link> to a statement prepared by <link
        linkend="rtn-oosqlPrepare">oosqlPrepare</link>. The handle can be null.
      </para>
      </listitem></varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Return value:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    Returns true if <link linkend="rtn-oosqlStep">oosqlStep</link> has been invoked at least once, and the statement has
    neither returned <computeroutput>DONE</computeroutput> nor been <link linkend="rtn-oosqlReset">reset</link>.
    Otherwise, and for a null handle, returns false.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Details</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The functionality of the <emphasis role="italic">oosqlStmtBusy</emphasis> routine is similar to that of the SQLite
    <ulink url="http://www.sqlite.org/c3ref/stmt_busy.html">sqlite3_stmt_busy</ulink> API.
  </para>
  <para>
    The <link linkend="mthStmtBusy">stmtBusy</link> method of the <link linkend="clsOOSQLiteStmt">ooSQLiteStmt</link>
    class does the same for a statement object.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Example:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    This example shows the value of <emphasis role="italic">oosqlStmtBusy</emphasis>, and of <link
    linkend="rtn-oosqlStmtReadonly">oosqlStmtReadonly</link>, while a query is executed:
<programlisting language="rexx">
  rc = oosqlPrepare(db, "SELECT name FROM foods;", 'stmt')
  say oosqlStmtReadonly(stmt) oosqlStmtBusy(stmt)    -- 1 0
  rc = oosqlStep(stmt)
  say oosqlStmtBusy(stmt)                            -- 1
  rc = oosqlReset(stmt)
  say oosqlStmtBusy(stmt)                            -- 0
  call oosqlFinalize stmt
</programlisting>
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</section>  <!-- End oosqlStmtBusy() -->


<section id="rtn-oosqlStmtReadonly"><title>oosqlStmtReadonly</title>
<indexterm><primary>oosqlStmtReadonly</primary></indexterm>
<indexterm><primary>Classic Rexx Interface</primary><secondary>oosqlStmtReadonly</secondary></indexterm>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject>
<imagedata fileref="images/classic/oosqlstmtreadonly.svg" scale="55" />
</imageobject>
</mediaobject>

<para>
  Tests if a prepared statement makes no direct changes to the database.
</para>
<variablelist>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Arguments:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The only argument is:
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry><term>stmt [required]</term>
      <listitem>
      <para>
        The <link linkend="defHandle">handle</link> to a statement prepared by <link
        linkend="rtn-oosqlPrepare">oosqlPrepare</link>. The handle must not be null, and the statement must not have
        been finalized: see the rules for <link linkend="sctClassicStmt">statement handles</link>.
      </para>
      </listitem></varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Return value:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    Returns true if the statement does not write to the database, such as a SELECT, and false if it can, such as an
    INSERT, an UPDATE, or a DELETE.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Remarks:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    A statement can be read only and still change the database indirectly, through a user defined function that executes
    SQL of its own. Transaction control statements, such as BEGIN and COMMIT, are read only, because they make no
    changes by themselves.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Details</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The functionality of the <emphasis role="italic">oosqlStmtReadonly</emphasis> routine is similar to that of the
    SQLite <ulink url="http://www.sqlite.org/c3ref/stmt_readonly.html">sqlite3_stmt_readonly</ulink> API.
  </para>
  <para>
    The <link linkend="mthstmtReadonly">stmtReadonly</link> method of the <link
    linkend="clsOOSQLiteStmt">ooSQLiteStmt</link> class does the same for a statement object.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Example:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    See the example of <link linkend="rtn-oosqlStmtBusy">oosqlStmtBusy</link>.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</section>  <!-- End oosqlStmtReadonly() -->


<section id="rtn-oosqlStmtStatus"><title>oosqlStmtStatus</title>
<indexterm><primary>oosqlStmtStatus</primary></indexterm>
<indexterm><primary>Classic Rexx Interface</primary><secondary>oosqlStmtStatus</secondary></indexterm>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject>
<imagedata fileref="images/classic/oosqlstmtstatus.svg" scale="55" />
</imageobject>
</mediaobject>

<para>
  Returns the value of one of the performance counters of a prepared statement.
</para>
<variablelist>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Arguments:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The arguments are:
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry><term>stmt [required]</term>
      <listitem>
      <para>
        The <link linkend="defHandle">handle</link> to a statement prepared by <link
        linkend="rtn-oosqlPrepare">oosqlPrepare</link>. The handle must not be null, and the statement must not have
        been finalized: see the rules for <link linkend="sctClassicStmt">statement handles</link>.
      </para>
      </listitem></varlistentry>
      <varlistentry><term>op [required]</term>
      <listitem>
      <para>
        The counter to return, one of the <link linkend="sctStatusParameterStmt">Status Parameter (stmt)</link>
        constants, such as <computeroutput>STMTSTATUS_FULLSCAN_STEP</computeroutput> or
        <computeroutput>STMTSTATUS_VM_STEP</computeroutput>.
      </para>
      </listitem></varlistentry>
      <varlistentry><term>reset [optional]</term>
      <listitem>
      <para>
        If true, the counter is set to 0 after its value is returned. The default is false.
      </para>
      </listitem></varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Return value:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The value of the counter. For an <emphasis role="italic">op</emphasis> that is not one of the constants, 0 is
    returned.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Remarks:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The counters accumulate over all the executions of the statement, also after a reset, until they are reset with the
    <emphasis role="italic">reset</emphasis> argument. They can help find out whether a query would benefit from an
    index.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Details</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    The functionality of the <emphasis role="italic">oosqlStmtStatus</emphasis> routine is similar to that of the SQLite
    <ulink url="http://www.sqlite.org/c3ref/stmt_status.html">sqlite3_stmt_status</ulink> API.
  </para>
  <para>
    The <link linkend="mthStmtStatus">stmtStatus</link> method of the <link
    linkend="clsOOSQLiteStmt">ooSQLiteStmt</link> class does the same for a statement object.
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
  <varlistentry><term><emphasis role="bold">Example:</emphasis></term>
  <listitem>
  <para>
    This example runs a query and displays some of its counters. There is no index on the price column, so the whole
    table is scanned, and the result has to be sorted:
<programlisting language="rexx">
  rc = oosqlPrepare(db, "SELECT name FROM foods WHERE price &gt; 1 ORDER BY name;", 'stmt')
  do while oosqlStep(stmt) == .ooSQLite~ROW
  end
  say 'Full scan steps:' oosqlStmtStatus(stmt, .ooSQLite~STMTSTATUS_FULLSCAN_STEP)
  say 'Sorts:          ' oosqlStmtStatus(stmt, .ooSQLite~STMTSTATUS_SORT)
  say 'VM steps:       ' oosqlStmtStatus(stmt, .ooSQLite~STMTSTATUS_VM_STEP, .true)
  say 'VM steps now:   ' oosqlStmtStatus(stmt, .ooSQLite~STMTSTATUS_VM_STEP)
  call oosqlFinalize stmt
  /* Output:
  Full scan steps: 3
  Sorts:           1
  VM steps:        47
  VM steps now:    0
  */
</programlisting>
  </para>
  </listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</section>  <!-- End oosqlStmtStatus() -->


